---
import Gallery from '../../layouts/Gallery.astro';
import Related from '../../components/Related.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const recipe = `<html data-density="compact">          <!-- app-wide default -->
…
<div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0" data-density="spacious">
  <!-- one table, locally more readable — density CASCADES -->
</div>

<script>
  // The complete persistence recipe:
  const d = localStorage.getItem('density') ?? 'comfortable';
  document.documentElement.dataset.density = d;
</script>`;
---
<Gallery title="Density">
  <p class="demo-note">
    ERP users live in dense grids, so density is a first-class dimension, not a
    theme afterthought: <code>data-density="compact | comfortable | spacious"</code>
    on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> or ANY wrapper remaps one token tier — rows go
    30 / 40 / 48px, controls 28 / 36 / 44px, and every component follows, because
    components consume <em>only</em> density aliases for internal sizing.
  </p>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Try it (this page is live)</h2>
    <p>The header's density selector drives this whole site. Per-region override:</p>
    <div class="bo-cluster" data-density="compact">
      <button class="bo-btn bo-btn--secondary">This cluster is compact</button>
      <span>regardless of the global setting — the attribute cascades.</span>
    </div>
    <pre><code>{recipe}</code></pre>
  </section>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Device/platform coverage</h2>
    <p>Audited 2026-08-15 as a follow-up to the Slice 7 Objective review — the
    question was whether "Web / Mobile app / RF / Tablet (Bento UI)" needs
    dedicated component variants. It doesn't: density + the named
    <a href={base + '/concepts/container-queries'}>container-query registry</a> already
    cover every one of these archetypes with the SAME components, just
    configured differently. No new CSS/JS came out of this — it's a
    verification pass, not a build.</p>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Archetype</th><th scope="col">Mechanism</th><th scope="col">Live example</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Web</td>
            <td><code>data-density="comfortable"</code> (the default) + ordinary container-query response</td>
            <td><a href={base + '/components/data-table'}>Data table</a>, <a href={base + '/components/dashboard'}>Dashboard</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Mobile app</td>
            <td><code>data-density="compact"</code> + the app shell's own <code>bo-shell</code> container query (sidebar collapses to an icon rail ≤ 56rem) — this docs site does exactly that</td>
            <td>Resize this page's window below 56rem, or see <a href={base + '/concepts/container-queries'}>the container-query registry</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Tablet (Bento UI)</td>
            <td><code>bo-widget-grid</code>'s container query (span-2 widgets collapse ≤ 41rem) — a Bento-style icon/tile grid is this SAME mechanism at a different breakpoint, not a new component. The forthcoming "App Launch" pattern (Slice 9, queued) uses it directly rather than inventing a separate grid.</td>
            <td><a href={base + '/components/dashboard'}>Dashboard</a> widget grid</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>RF / warehouse</td>
            <td><code>data-density="spacious"</code> (44px <em>rows</em> — the control boxes inside them do not grow; see the target-size note below) + <code>initScanInput()</code>'s keyboard-wedge input mode — the one archetype that genuinely needed a new input pattern, not just a layout setting</td>
            <td><a href={base + '/patterns/goods-receipt'}>Goods receipt pattern</a></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><strong>Open question, not resolved here</strong>: whether
    RF/Mobile/Tablet are real target markets worth more investment than this
    documentation pass is a product decision, not something a coverage audit can
    settle — the recommendation is to keep documenting the mechanism regardless,
    since the cost is low and the value doesn't depend on the answer.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Rules that keep density safe</h2>
    <ul class="bo-stack bo-stack--tight docs-list">
      <li>Density tokens are <strong>rem-only</strong> — they scale with the user's root font, so raised text never clips. The low-vision power user runs large text + compact and gets density at a readable size; that combination is the point.</li>
      <li>Explicit <code>data-density</code> beats container-query
      auto-compaction <strong>when set on the table or its container
      element itself</strong> — a distant wrapper's density tokens still
      inherit, but the compaction query only checks those two elements,
      so it stays in force. (This sentence is the canonical statement;
      2026-08-21 sweep — two pages previously said "always beats", which
      the shipped <code>:not([data-density])</code> selector contradicts.)</li>
      <li><strong>Density moves row height and font size, not control boxes.</strong>
      A checkbox, radio and the tag-input remove button are <code>1rem</code>
      (16px) in <em>every</em> tier, and the data-table sort button is 18px
      tall. WCAG 2.5.8 is met through its <strong>spacing exception</strong> —
      a 24px circle centred on each of those reaches no other target, and
      <code>check:target-size</code> runs that exact spec test in CI across
      all three tiers — <em>not</em> through a 24px size floor. If you pack controls
      closer than 24px between centres, you break that, and the framework
      cannot guarantee it for markup it did not author.</li>
      <li>Font size and row height move <em>together</em> per tier — never mix a density font token with a hand-set height.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <Related links={[["/concepts/tokens", "Design tokens"], ["/concepts/container-queries", "Container queries"]]} />
</Gallery>
